speechtext:
However, I do not think the minister can very soundly argue that the Edmonton mayor or the Edmonton city council were satisfied with the treatment the men received in respect to the subway work last winter. Neither do I think they are satisfied with the treatment they have had in the whole work that was undertaken to relieve unemployment. In fact I have before me a resolution recently adopted by the Edmonton city council, and I hope the Minister of Railways will do me the honour of listening to it. It reads:
Whereas the problem of unemployment is one that is generated or abated by the policies or lack of policy of the federal government;
And whereas the burden placed on this municipality by taking care of the unemployed in this district is inequitable and unfair to the taxpayers of this city;
And whereas three members of the House of Commons represent constituencies of which this city is a part;
Therefore be it resolved that this city council instruct the city clerk to forward to the members representing the Edmonton constituencies an outline of the obligations undertaken on behalf of the unemployed by this city, with the request that they introduce at this session measures that will obligate the federal government to assume its fair share of the costs of relief work.
I take it from that that the Edmonton city council are not quite satisfied with the treatment they have received in connection with this relief for the unemployed. I also have
before me a statement of what has been spent by the city in relief work. They have spent $111,715 this past winter, and added to that is $1,500, two-thirds of which went to the Welfare board and $500 to the Salvation Army to look after emergency cases.
When we were discussing this matter a little while ago the Acting Minister of Finance (Mr. Robb) stated that in his opinion there was very little unemployment in Canada, and his speech rather suggested that if there was any it was probably Conservative propaganda to affect the coming election. I hope such an insinuation will not be reflected against the council of the city of Edmonton because the mayor got very lenient treatment indeed from the government last year when he was negotiating with the Woodward elevator people at Vancouver. He is at the present time a candidate for election to the federal House and a supporter of the government in East Edmonton constituency. He stated only last week through the press there that he intended to run as a supporter of the King government in the next election. Then they also have an alderman there, Mr. Joseph Clarke, who, I understand, makes the boast that he was a playmate in his school days of the present Prime Minister and that he also will be a candidate in East Edmonton at the next election.
